==Old English==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|gem-pro|ang}} {{m|gem-pro|*fuglaz}}. Cognate with Old Frisian {{term|fugel|lang=ofs}} (West Frisian {{term|fûgel|lang=fy}}), Old High German {{term|fogal|lang=goh}} (German {{term|Vogel|lang=de}}), Old Saxon {{term|fugal|lang=osx}} (New Saxon (Low German) {{term|Vagel|Vagel, Vogel|lang=nds}}), Old Dutch {{term|fogal|lang=odt}} (Dutch {{term|vogel|lang=nl}}), Old Norse {{term|fugl|lang=non}} (Swedish {{term|fågel|lang=sv}}, Danish {{term|fugl|lang=da}}), Gothic {{term|𐍆𐌿𐌲𐌻𐍃|tr=fugls|lang=got}}. The Germanic base is a variant of {{m|gem-pro|*flugla-||fly}}, compare {{m|gem-pro|*fleuganą}} (Old English {{m|ang|flēogan}}).

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈfuɡol/|[ˈfuɣol]|lang=ang}}

